Add 1 to a Number Represented by a Linked List

Problem Statement
You are given the head of a singly linked list representing a positive integer. Each node of the linked list represents a single digit of the number, with the first node containing the most significant digit (the leftmost digit) and the last node containing the least significant digit (the rightmost digit).

Your task is to add 1 to the integer value represented by the linked list and return the head of the modified linked list.

Note: The number will not contain any leading zeroes, unless the number itself is exactly 0.

Examples
Example 1:

Input: head = [4, 5, 6]  (representing the number 456)
Output: [4, 5, 7]
Explanation: 456 + 1 = 457. The modified linked list is 4 -> 5 -> 7.

Example 2:

Input: head = [9, 9, 9]  (representing the number 999)
Output: [1, 0, 0, 0]
Explanation: 999 + 1 = 1000. We carry the 1 all the way to the front, requiring a new head node. The modified linked list is 1 -> 0 -> 0 -> 0.

Example 3:

Input: head = [0] (representing the number 0)
Output: [1]
Explanation: 0 + 1 = 1. The modified linked list is 1.

Constraints:
1 <= N <= 10^5 (N is the number of node is ll)
0 <= Node.data <= 9
The linked list represents a valid number (no leading zeros except for the number o itself)

Creating a standard Node class:
class Node {
public: 
    int data;
    Node* next;

    Node(int value) {
        data = value;
        next = nullptr;
    }
};

1. Iterative approach using Reversal
Because a singly linked list only allows us to traverse forward (from the most significant digit to least significant digit), adding numbers directly is difficult without knowing the carry from the end.
To fix this we can reverse the list, do the addition starting from the head (which is now the least significant digit) and then reverse the list back.

class Solution {
private:
    //Helper function to reverse the linked list 
    Node* reverseList(Node* head) {
        Node* prev = nullptr;
        Node* current = head;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            Node* nextNode = current->next;
            current->next = prev;
            prev = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }

        return prev;
    }

public: 
    Node* addOne(Node* head) {
        //Step1: Reverse the list to make the least significant digit accessible 
        head = reverseList(head);

        Node* current = head;
        int carry = 1; //We start with a carry of 1 because we want to add 1

        //Step 2: Traverse the reversed list and add the carry 
        while (current != nullptr && carry > 0) {
            int sum = current->data + carry;
            current->data = sum % 10;
            carry = sum / 10;

            //If we are at the last node and still have a carry, append a new node 
            if (current->next == nullptr && carry > 0) {
                current->next = new Node(carry);
                carry = 0; //Carry is consumed 
            }
            current = current->next;
        }

        //Step 3: Reverse the list back to its original order 
        return reverseList(head);
    }
    
};

Iterative:

TC: O(N)
We traverse the list 3 times.
Once to reverse it.
Once to add the carry.
Once to reverse it back.

SC: O(1)

Pros: No extra space needed 
Cons: Tampering data and more time taken 

Recursive Approach (Using Backtracking)
If you want to avlid reversing the list, recursion is the way to solve this question. 
The recursive call traverses all the way to the end of the list. On its way back up 
(during the backtracking phase), it adds the carry (which starts at 1 at the very end).

class Solution {
private:
    //Recursive function to add 1 and return the carry 
    int addOneUntil(Node* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return 1;
        }

        //Recurse to the very end of the list 
        int carry = addOneUntil(node->next);

        //Backtracking step: Add the carry returned from the right to the current node 
        int sum = node->data + carry;
        node->data = sum % 10;

        //Return the new carry to the previous node in the call stack 
        return sum / 10;
    }

public: 
    Node* addOne(Node* head) {

        //Perform recursive addition 
        int finalCarry = addOneUntil(head);

        //If a carry remains after processing the original head (eg: 999 + 1)
        //we must create a new head node (1 -> 0 -> 0 -> 0)
        if (finalCarry > 0) {
            Node* newHead = new Node(finalCarry);
            newHead->next = head;
            head = newHead;
        }

        return head;
    }
};

TC: O(N)
The recursive function visits each node in the linked list exactly once to reach the end
and processes each node exactly once while backtracking.

SC: O(N)
Coz we traverse to the very end of ll before returning the recursive call stack will grow to size N.
Therefore it takes O(N) auxiliary space.

